claims=[]
def c(n,ns,loc,words,statement,status,comp,cl,fate,dates,present,refs,confidence='high'):
    claims.append(dict(id=f'CL-SA09-138E-{n:03}',items=['SA09-'+str(104+x) for x in ns],cote=138,scans=sorted(set(x[0] for x in loc)),lines=[f'{TF}:{x[1]}-{x[2]}' for x in loc],his_statement=words,statement=statement,status_on_pages=status,computation=comp,**{'class':cl},fate=fate,fate_date=dates,present_status='As of 2026-11-19: '+present,refs=refs,confidence=confidence))

c(1,[4],[(5,141,170),(7,255,259)],'«1→mathbb G→mathcal G→Γ»; «image inverse de … Γ»','For a connected ks-scheme X for which ks is the separable algebraic closure of k in H⁰(X,O_X), every k-automorphism preserves ks and induces an element of Γ=Gal(ks/k); its kernel is Aut_ks(X). For k⊂k1⊂ks, Aut_k1(X) is the inverse image of Γ1=Gal(ks/k1). The displayed sequence is not asserted surjective. Connected finite étale covering objects preserve the required constant-field condition.','proof sketched',{'method':'Restriction to the intrinsic separable constant subfield; kernel and inverse-image calculation.','output':'No numerical computation. The Γ rather than Γ1 in line166 is inconsistent with its surrounding diagram and is a scan alarm.'},'E','This is the elementary semilinear automorphism and Galois-descent dictionary, already underlying SGA1 VIII. The exact line166 reading is not silently emended. No claim of a new later theorem is needed.','SGA1 seminar1960–1961; publication1971; exact manuscript composition date unknown.','Correct with the displayed constant-field hypothesis and Γ1 correction; no assertion that every field automorphism preserves the isomorphism class of X.',['SGA1: Exposé VIII §§1–5'], 'high')
c(2,[5],[(5,174,187),(6,189,207)],'«Une donnée de descente … revient à un scindage»; «sous réserve … d’effectivité»','An admissible continuous semilinear Γ-action on a ks-scheme is descent data; for an intermediate field replace Γ by its closed subgroup. When descent is effective, a chosen action corresponds to a k-model with a chosen identification after scalar extension. Quasi-projectivity supplies the customary effectiveness criterion after finite presentation/continuity reduction. An arbitrary abstract section is not asserted effective.','stated without proof',{'method':'Checked cocycle identity against composition and the manuscript’s explicit effectiveness qualification.','output':'No numeric computation.'},'E','SGA1 VIII develops descent data and proves effective descent for quasi-projective schemes in Corollaire7.7; Proposition7.8 treats descent with ample invertible sheaves. The finite/infinite field and continuity restrictions must be retained.','Seminar1960–1961; SGA1 publication1971.','Established descent theory. No unresolved conjecture is created by the source’s caveat.',['SGA1: VIII Corollaire7.7 and Proposition7.8, PDFpp189–190'], 'high')
c(3,[6],[(6,209,223)],'«rev. universel (pro-fini)»; «1→π1→mathcal E→mathcal G→1»','For a connected locally noetherian geometric scheme X with its universal pro-étale finite-cover object, the group E of compatible automorphisms of that object over automorphisms of X fits into 1→π₁(X)→E→Aut_k(X)→1. A lift exists because pulling back the universal covering object gives an isomorphic universal object; the induced outer action respects the profinite topology. E need not itself be profinite when the automorphism quotient is infinite/noncompact.','proof sketched',{'method':'Galois-category lifting and kernel argument.','output':'No numeric computation; underlying Galois-category hypotheses are stated explicitly as modernization.'},'E','SGA1 V gives the Galois-category equivalence and automorphism/fibre-functor construction; IX6.1 gives the arithmetic exact sequence for a geometrically connected quasi-compact scheme over a field. The latter is a related special case, not an exact citation for arbitrary Aut_k(X).','Seminar1960–1961; publication1971.','Correct in the stated Galois-category setting; topology and representability of the pro-object should not be suppressed.',['SGA1: V §§4–7; IX Théorème6.1'], 'high')
c(4,[7],[(6,227,232),(7,235,262)],'«mathcal E′=Norm_mathcal E(N)»; «mathcal G′=mathcal E′/N»','For a closed normal N⊂π₁(X), the compatible automorphism group of the Galois pro-cover X′=X̃/N is N_E(N)/N. It surjects onto Aut_k(X) precisely when N is invariant under E, equivalently under the induced outer action on closed normal subgroups. The geometric deck group is π₁(X)/N.','proof sketched',{'method':'An automorphism descends iff it preserves the equivalence relation defined by N; the kernel is N.','output':'Independent group-theoretic verification in synthesis.md; no exhaustive computation required.'},'E','This is the stabilizer/normalizer form of the covering-subgroup correspondence in SGA1 V. Later arithmetic applications do not alter the exact normalizer criterion.','Seminar1960–1961; publication1971.','Established; the text correctly retracts unrestricted lifting for arbitrary Galois covers.',['SGA1: V Galois categories and connected Galois covers'], 'high')
c(5,[9],[(7,264,264)],'«Si π1 est … de type fini … stables par tt autom continu»','Every topologically finitely generated profinite group has a basis of open characteristic subgroups: intersect all open normal subgroups of index≤m. There are finitely many because a continuous map to each finite symmetric group is determined by finitely many generators.','stated without proof',{'method':'Independent proof using finitely many homomorphisms into S_m and taking finite intersections.','output':'Full proof in synthesis.md; no code needed.'},'E','This is the standard finiteness argument for finitely generated profinite groups. The opened Haran–Jarden–Pop author manuscript states the exact characteristic-basis construction at the start of§1 and cites Fried–Jarden Lemma16.10.2. The first printed occurrence was not established; no false priority date is supplied. The proof, rather than a title match, settles the exact statement.','Classical profinite-group argument; first publication not determined in this search.','True; finite generation is essential to the finite-intersection argument.',['HaranJardenPop2009: §1 opening paragraph, printedp6, citing Fried–Jarden Lemma16.10.2'], 'high')
c(6,[10],[(8,279,296)],'«même image» [replaces «surjectif»]','For the pair automorphism map G′→G with common arithmetic target Γ, surjectivity is equivalent to surjectivity on geometric kernels together with equality of arithmetic images. The kernel Aut_ks(X′→X) is the inverse image of Aut_ks(X).','corrected by himself later (both places): scan8 line296 deletes “surjectif” onto Γ and substitutes “même image”, while retaining geometric-kernel surjectivity',{'method':'Diagram chase: lift arithmetic image, then correct the remaining geometric-kernel element.','output':'No computation.'},'E','An elementary exact-sequence criterion, already part of the standard descent framework. The surviving statement is stronger textually than a loose paraphrase requiring full image Γ: equality of two possibly proper images suffices.','Standard before this manuscript; precise first publication not determined.','Correct after the visible self-correction.',['SGA1: VIII descent framework'], 'high')
c(7,[11],[(8,300,300),(10,355,355)],'«de prés. finie … l’image … sous-groupe ouvert»','A finite-presentation ks-scheme, and any finite-presentation morphism between such schemes, are defined over a finite subextension of ks/k. Thus their semilinear automorphism images in Γ contain an open subgroup. “Finite presentation over k” for the geometric scheme is not the intended usable hypothesis when ks/k is infinite; the source has an uncertain base symbol in scan8 and k in scan10.','stated without proof',{'method':'Finitely many equations/gluing data and relations use finitely many algebraic coefficients; finite-presentation Hom descent similarly gives eventual equality.','output':'Independent finite-data argument; EGA locator recorded separately.'},'E','EGA IV3 §8.8 provides the finite-presentation limit theorem (8.8.2). The original PDF was downloaded after browser extraction failed; §8.8, including the theorem, proof, Corollaires8.8.2.4–5 and Scholie8.8.3, was read in full. These exact hypotheses agree with the finite-field-of-definition application.','EGA IV3 published1966; original received15Nov1964 according to Numdam metadata.','True with ks base and finite-presentation hypotheses. The literal k reading remains for scan review.',['EGAIV3: Théorème8.8.2, Corollaires8.8.2.4–5, Scholie8.8.3, printedpp28–33','SGA1: IX6.1 proof'], 'high')
c(8,[12,68],[(9,304,339)],'«une donnée de descente … scindage partiel»; «oubli à réparer ds rédaction au propre!»','For the diagram X′→X, descent data to k1 are admissible sections of the pair semilinear automorphism group over Γ1. Its inverse-image and intersection groups satisfy E′_1=E′∩E_1 and G′_1=Aut_k1(X′→X). The manuscript’s repair flag does not say which missing term or condition was intended.','open on the pages',{'method':'Cocycle and inverse-image verification for the explicit formulas; no reconstruction of the unspecified omission.','output':'The flagged omission is indexed as MP-SA09-40; the possibly missing prime is a scan question.'},'C','The explicit diagram-descent construction is standard SGA1 VIII, but a literature search cannot identify the precise unpublished omission. What remains unresolved is textual identification of the repair, not a conjectural failure of descent theory.','SGA1 seminar1960–1961/publication1971; no date for resolution of the unspecified manuscript repair.','Mathematical framework established; the author’s exact repair remains unidentifiable without the ink or a clean revision.',['SGA1: VIII §§1–5, Corollaire7.7'], 'high')
c(9,[13],[(10,342,355),(11,357,372)],'«un groupe quotient fini … dont l’extension … provienne … par image inverse»','For a profinite extension 1→H→E→Γ→1 with finite H, choose an open normal U⊂E with U∩H=1. Then Γ_i=p(U) is open normal, U≅Γ_i, and E≅(E/U)×_(Γ/Γ_i)Γ. Thus a normal partial section exists after shrinking. A prescribed partial section need not have normal image merely because it centralizes H.','stated without proof',{'method':'Independent fibre-product proof: the canonical map is injective by U∩H=1 and surjective by adjusting a lift with U.','output':'Detailed proof and distinction from claim010 in synthesis.md.'},'E','The finite-kernel extension reduction is an elementary compact/profinite-group argument. It validates the existence sentence even though the subsequent equivalence for a fixed section is wrong. Cohomological finite-quotient descriptions give the same conclusion with continuous data.','Classical profinite-group background; specific first printed occurrence not established.','True for a profinite extension (the finite geometric-automorphism situation supplies this topology); see corrected fixed-section criterion in claim010.',['MilneCFT2020: II §4 continuous cohomology; carried as background'], 'high')
c(10,[14],[(11,365,372)],'«image … ss-groupe invariant … ce qui revient … commute à mathbb G′»','The source equates normality in E of a partial section s(Γ_i) with centralizing the finite kernel H. Normality implies centralization, but centralization only gives normality in p⁻¹(Γ_i). For normality in all E one additionally needs E-conjugation compatibility: e s(γ)e⁻¹=s(p(e)γp(e)⁻¹).','false as stated',{'program':'programs/computations.py','output':'outputs/computations.json:C02,C03','counterexample':'D8 finite witness and a geometric witness satisfying the surrounding hypotheses: X=P¹_Qbar minus S, S={−1,−1/2,−1/4,1/2,2/3,4/5,2,3,5}, X′=X, Aut_Qbar(X)=C3, all automorphisms rational. Hence E=C3×G_Q. Γ_i=G_Q(√−3); its quotient Gal(Q(√−3,∛2)/Q(√−3))=C3 defines c. Graph s=(c,id) centralizes H=C3 but conjugation by complex conjugation changes c to c⁻¹, so its image is not normal. The504 Möbius candidates are exhaustively checked over exact fractions; no scan emendation is assumed.','refined_statement_id':'CL-SA09-138E-009'},'C','No later paper is needed to refute the exact equivalence: the explicit counterexample does so. The corrected finite-quotient existence statement is claim009. A targeted search in descent/centralizer literature did not identify a publication of this specific draft correction; this is not evidence of novelty.','Refutation and refined proof in this run,2026-11-19; antecedent group theory classical.','False under a fixed-section reading, even for the actual geometric finite-kernel/Γ infinite setting. The issue cannot be dismissed as an abstract group example outside the manuscript’s scope.',['MilneCFT2020: II Example1.18, background only'], 'high')
c(11,[15],[(12,378,386)],'«groupes finis étales»; «autom. géom … définis sur ki»; «s’égaliser … surextension finie»','For a finite geometric automorphism group with its continuous Galois action, the corresponding finite étale group scheme becomes constant over a finite extension. Any fixed finite-presentation pair and finitely many geometric automorphisms descend after finite extension. Two chosen finite descents with the same geometric identification become equal after further finite extension. This asserts finite étale descent of the discrete automorphism group, not automatic representability/reducedness of an arbitrary full automorphism functor.','stated without proof',{'method':'Finite continuous Galois action has open kernel; finite-presentation morphisms descend and eventually agree.','output':'No numeric computation.'},'E','Finite étale group schemes are equivalent to finite groups with continuous Galois action (SGA1 V/IX); eventual definition and agreement are finite-presentation limit arguments. The distinction between the finite group and a potentially nonreduced automorphism functor is required in positive characteristic.','SGA1 seminar1960–1961/publication1971; EGA IV3 publication1966.','True in the discrete finite-automorphism setting explicitly used by the arithmetic extension.',['SGA1: V and IX6.1','EGAIV3:8.8.2 and Corollaires8.8.2.4–5, printedpp28–33'], 'high')
c(12,[16],[(12,388,390),(13,393,416)],'«z=Z∩mathbb G′=Centre de mathbb G′»; «Si z=1 … existence ssi Γi⊂Γ0»','Let H be finite normal in profinite E and Z=C_E(H). Then Z∩H=Z(H)=z, its image Γ0 in Γ is open, and 1→z→Z→Γ0→1 is central. If z=1, restriction gives a unique section Γ_i→Z exactly for Γ_i⊂Γ0. These are centralizing sections; global normality of their images remains the separate issue in claim010.','proof sketched',{'method':'Conjugation E→Aut(H) has open kernel; intersection identifies z. With z=1, compact-to-Hausdorff continuous bijection Z→Γ0 is an isomorphism.','output':'Independent proof; no numerical computation.'},'E','Classical finite-group/profinite centralizer reasoning. Continuous group cohomology, recorded in Milne II §§1,4, supplies the next obstruction formulation. No later novelty is claimed.','Standard group theory before the manuscript; first source for this exact phrasing not identified.','Correct, with solutions interpreted as centralizing sections rather than globally normal sections.',['MilneCFT2020: II Example1.18 and §4'], 'high')
c(13,[17],[(14,418,422)],'«obstruction … H²(Γi,z) … indétermination … H¹=Hom»','For the restricted central profinite extension over Γ_i⊂Γ0 with finite discrete trivial Γ_i-module z, the continuous H² class vanishes iff a continuous section exists; when nonempty, the set of sections is a torsor under H¹_cont(Γ_i,z)=Hom_cont(Γ_i,z). The H²/H¹ statement concerns centralizing sections, not the missing ambient-conjugation invariance.','stated without proof',{'method':'Choose a continuous set-theoretic section after finite quotient reduction; its factor set is a continuous2-cocycle. Changing lifts adds a coboundary; differences of homomorphic sections are continuous homomorphisms.','output':'Independent targeted cohomological check in synthesis.md.'},'E','Milne’s opened exposition II Example1.18 gives the crossed-homomorphism and extension classifications; §4 gives the continuous profinite version. These are classical cohomological facts, not a later resolution of a new manuscript conjecture.','Classical pre-manuscript cohomology; opened exposition version4.03,2020, firstwebversion1996.','Correct for central finite coefficients and continuous sections.',['MilneCFT2020: II Example1.18(a,b), pp65–66; II §4'], 'high')
c(14,[18],[(15,424,443)],'«0 ou1 points»; «tout rev. étale … trivial»','Over an algebraically closed characteristic-zero field, P¹ and A¹ have no nontrivial connected finite étale covers. Over Qbar a finite-presentation cover descends to a number field and the complex comparison proves this. In positive characteristic A¹ has Artin–Schreier covers, so the field restriction is material.','stated without proof',{'method':'Complex sphere and plane are simply connected; SGA1 comparison transfers finite covers.','output':'No numerical computation.'},'E','SGA1 XII Théorème5.1 and Corollaire5.2 identify finite étale covers with finite analytic covers and étaleπ1 with the profinite completion of topologicalπ1. XI§1 treats projective spaces.','SGA1 seminar1960–1961; publication1971.','True as written after the source restricts to characteristic zero; the later blanket prime-field extension does not include the unqualified A¹ assertion.',['SGA1: XI§1; XII5.1–5.2'], 'high')
c(15,[19,20],[(15,445,471),(16,473,487)],'«rev. par z↦zⁿ»; «Γ⋉Qbar*»','Over an algebraically closed characteristic-zero field every connected finite étale cover of G_m is a power map after choices of coordinates. Its deck group is μ_n; the geometric universal-cover kernel is T=lim μ_n, while the group lifting translations is lim_(power maps)Qbar*. The unrestricted automorphism group of G_m also includes inversion z↦z⁻¹, so the written Γ⋉Qbar* is correct only if the two deleted points are ordered.','false as stated',{'program':'programs/computations.py','output':'outputs/computations.json:C15','method':'The power-cover classification follows from π1(C*)=Z; inversion is an explicit missing automorphism. The inverse-limit translation group and Tate kernel are distinguished by projection to the base coordinate.'},'C','The corrected cover classification is classical and follows from SGA1 XII5.1–5.2; the full automorphism group is G_m⋊C2 (and semilinear Γ). The omitted marking condition is a source/scope issue, not an open classification problem.','Classical complex covering theory; SGA1 publication1971.','Power maps and pro-cover correct; group formula needs ordered punctures or the inversion factor.',['SGA1: XII5.1–5.2'], 'high')
c(16,[21],[(16,488,497)],'«3points … Aut=mathfrak S3 … forme canonique surQ»','An ordered triple of distinct geometric points on P¹ has a unique Möbius normalization to (0,1,∞); the unordered three-point marked curve has geometric automorphism group S3 and a canonical Q-isomorphism class represented by P¹−{0,1,∞}. There is no unique chosen normalization without an ordering. The Fermat curves provide its standard rank-two abelian cover tower.','stated without proof',{'method':'Unique3-transitivity of PGL₂; the six anharmonic transformations; Fermat tower checked in claims025–029.','output':'C01 invariance computations also check the sixfold quotient generator.'},'E','Classical Möbius geometry and covering theory; SGA1 XII supplies the algebraic/analytic cover equivalence. Esquisse§3 later uses the marked three-point line for dessins, but does not originate this normalization.','Classical before the manuscript; Esquissecirculated1984,published1997.','True with the distinction between a canonical isomorphism class and a chosen coordinate.',['SGA1: XII5.1–5.2','Esquisse1984: §3'], 'high')
c(17,[22],[(16,498,501),(17,503,506)],'«corps K … rapports anharmoniques … indépendant du choix»','For a finite set S⊂P¹(Qbar) of at least3 distinct points, normalize any chosen ordered triple to0,1,∞ and let K be generated overQ by all remaining coordinates. Changing the triple uses rational fractional-linear formulas in those coordinates, so K is unchanged. This is the minimum field over which all labelled points can be rational after such normalization; all setwise Möbius automorphisms are then defined overK. It is not generally the unlabelled field of moduli.','proof sketched',{'method':'Both triple changes and their inverses have coefficients rational in normalized coordinates; compare generated fields in both directions.','output':'Independent algebraic proof in synthesis.md; no numeric computation.'},'E','This is classical cross-ratio normalization. Search of moduli/descent literature was used to guard against equating the labelled coordinate field with the unlabelled field of moduli; no such equality is claimed.','Classical invariant theory before the manuscript; exact earliest occurrence not determined.','Correct with labelled-rationality and chosen-identification qualifications.',['SGA1: VIII descent, background only'], 'high')
c(18,[23],[(19,526,585)],'«K_n=Q[μ_6n]»; «ν est l’ordre … de2 … K_n*/(K_n*)ⁿ»','For z↦zⁿ in characteristic zero, the nonzero special fibres are μ_n, nth roots of−j and−jbar, and nth roots of−1,1/2,2. The union of the first three cyclotomic cosets gives roots of z^(3n)=−1 and field Q(μ_6n). Over a field containing μ_n, adjoining one nth root of2 has degree equal to the order of[2] in K*/K*ⁿ. Q[√[n]2] alone is not a Galois splitting field unless enough roots of unity are also adjoined.','proof sketched',{'program':'programs/computations.py','output':'outputs/computations.json:C04,C17','method':'Power/coset identities are direct; finite Kummer degree is verified via the perfect Kummer pairing, not inferred from numerical samples.'},'E','Finite Kummer theory is classical. Milne VII AppendixA states the radical-extension correspondence; LMS2014§2 and Schinzel1977 provide the exact radical/cyclotomic entanglement needed for later image questions. The source’s ambiguous bracket notation is not promoted into a false Galois claim.','Classical Kummer theory; Schinzel1977; LMSfirstpreprint2011,published2014.','Correct after distinguishing a chosen radical field from its cyclotomic normal closure.',['MilneCFT2020: VII AppendixA','LMS2014: §2','Schinzel1977: bibliography verified; fulltext blocked'], 'high')

c(19,[25],[(19,588,600)],'«27z²(z−1)² / 4(z²−z+1)³»','In characteristic zero, g(z)=27z²(z−1)²/[4(z²−z+1)³] is invariant under z↦1−z and z↦1/z and is the degree-six quotient by S3. Its ramification partitions at0,1,∞ are (2,2,2),(2,2,2),(3,3). The correct identity is 4(z²−z+1)³−27z²(z−1)²=(z+1)²(2z−1)²(z−2)². Several displayed intermediate factors omit z or a normalizing factor.','false as stated',{'program':'programs/computations.py','output':'outputs/computations.json:C01','method':'Exact symbolic invariance, derivative, factorization, residuals and Riemann–Hurwitz sum10=2·6−2; the main rational map is correct, intermediate literal equalities are false.'},'C','The corrected quotient is the classical anharmonic quotient (a normalized reciprocal of the Legendre j-invariant). Search matched the invariant construction but no publication specifically repairing these draft intermediate lines. The exact polynomial certificate settles the calculation without treating a typographic conjecture as ink evidence.','Classical invariant theory; explicit draft-error check2026-11-19; first occurrence not determined.','Main map and ramification correct; intermediate factor lines require scan review.',['SGA1: XII5.1–5.2, comparison background','Esquisse1984: §3, dessin context only'], 'high')
c(20,[26],[(20,604,649)],'«image de dim n+1?»; «Z-indép. mod racines de1»; «image d’indice fini???»','For multiplicatively independent nonzero rational numbers ξ1,…,ξr modulo roots of unity, G_Q acts on all compatible radicals through Zhat^r⋊Zhat*. The image is open. In the intrinsic automorphism group of the saturated rational radical group, its index is exactly2^r. On an ℓ-adic factor the Lie dimension is r+1; “dimension” of the whole adelic group must not be mistaken for algebraic dimension of a finite group. A finite Galois-stable set D and its restriction-of-scalars torus require passage to a splitting number field before scalarizing the linear action.','open on the pages',{'method':'Full-rank theorem matched to the saturated radical group; change from original independent generators has finite index. The rank-one obstruction is independently computed in C04.','program':'programs/computations.py','output':'outputs/computations.json:C04','limit':'This computation illustrates rank1 only; the general-rank theorem is not claimed proved by samples.'},'B','Moree–Stevenhagen2014 Theorem2.5 proves the precise2^r index for Γ⊂Q* of rankr, with A=Aut_(Γ∞∩Q*)Γ∞; its§2 construction gives the finite-index affine assertion. Later number-field/torus work is relevant to the non-split-torus interpretation, but the exact opened theorem settles the explicitly rational split case. Schinzel1977 is a verified bibliographic ancestor through LMS2014; the historical first settlement of all intended torus variants is not established here.','Moree–Stevenhagen preprint20Mar2012, revision29Dec2013, publication2014; Schinzel1977 antecedent, not equated with the modern theorem.','Rational split rankr question settled, with the precise saturation qualification. The imprecise general torus sketch cannot be assigned the same exact2^r formula.',['MoreeStevenhagen2014: §2, Theorem2.5','LMS2014: §2','PerissinottoPerucca2023: abstract and bibliography, contextual extension'], 'high')
c(21,[27,28,30],[(21,653,687),(22,704,729)],'«Q*/Q*ⁿ≃H¹(Γ,Z/nZ)»; «…⊕Z/nZ≃Z¹»; «Hom» [deleted]→«Z¹»','Kummer theory canonically gives H¹(G_Q,μ_n)≅Q*/Q*ⁿ, with cyclotomic action on μ_n, and 0→μ_n/μ_n(Q)→Z¹→H¹→0. A choice of root changes a cocycle by a coboundary. At the full Tate limit H¹(G_Q,Zhat(1))≅limQ*/Q*ⁿ and B¹≅Zhat(1), but the extension does not split as abelian groups: Z¹ is torsion-free while H¹ contains the order-two class[−1]. Neither an untwisted coefficient identification nor the written direct-sum formulas are generally correct.','false as stated',{'program':'programs/computations.py','output':'outputs/computations.json:C05,C16','method':'At n=2 every coboundary vanishes, contradicting an added Z/2 kernel overH¹. At the inverse limit, pointwise torsion-freeness of Zhat(1) makes Z¹ torsion-free; [−1] remains nonzero of order2 in the Kummer completion. Compatible finite roots and the finite invariant Mittag–Leffler condition justify H¹=limH¹. The non-split conclusion is independently proved, not inferred from finite samples.'},'C','The corrected Kummer and cocycle statements are classical; Milne II Example1.18 and Hilbert90 plus VII AppendixA verify their ingredients. The author visibly replaces Hom by crossed homomorphisms, but the surviving coefficients and split sum still need correction. No later theorem can validate the literal torsion contradiction.','Classical Kummer/cohomology before manuscript; this run’s literal-formula refutation2026-11-19.','Canonical H¹ assertion correct with μ_n twist; literal Z¹ direct sums false, at finite level as compatible extensions and at inverse level even as abstract abelian groups.',['MilneCFT2020: II Example1.18, Proposition1.22; VII AppendixA'], 'high')
c(22,[29],[(21,685,687),(22,704,719)],'«lim (Z/nZ)^P … Zhat^P»','Writing Q*≅{±1}×⊕_pZ, its n-power completion is {±1}×{(a_p)∈∏_pZhat: for every n, a_p≡0 modn for all but finitely many p}. It is not the unrestricted product∏_pZhat. The all-ones tuple fails already mod2; the tuple whose successive coordinates are1!,2!,3!,… has infinite support but does satisfy the restriction.','false as stated',{'program':'programs/computations.py','output':'outputs/computations.json:C06','method':'Inverse limits of the finitely supported residue groups characterize the displayed restricted subset in both directions; factorial examples illustrate the proof.'},'C','The rational-prime decomposition and finite Kummer quotients are classical (Milne VIIA.4); inverse-limit definitions give the exact repair. Search found no source making the unrestricted-product formula valid under these quantifiers. This is the n-power completion, not an assertion about every possible profinite completion of the infinitely generated group.','Classical algebra; explicit draft correction2026-11-19.','Unrestricted product false; the restricted inverse-limit group is established by the independent proof.',['MilneCFT2020: VII AppendixA, ExampleA.4'], 'high')
c(23,[30],[(23,745,760)],'«splitté, splitting dépend du choix de √[ν]2»; Γ′ identification deleted','Choosing an origin of a radical torsor splits its full affine automorphism group as translations⋊linear automorphisms. It does not in general split the arithmetic Galois image onto its cyclotomic quotient. The source cancels one Γ′ identification and leaves several finite-level groups ambiguous; the marginal splitting cannot unambiguously be assigned to an arithmetic or ambient extension.','open on the pages',{'program':'programs/computations.py','output':'outputs/computations.json:C04,C16','method':'Affine-origin computation correct. LMS2014§2 explicitly distinguishes split ambientA from its non-split arithmeticG extension by2T. No unqualified theorem is extracted from the ambiguous diagram.'},'F','LMS2014§2 settles the precisely defined modern rank-one groups and records non-splitting of the arithmetic extension. The page’s exact choice of Γ′ and ν is not identifiable enough to decide whether the marginal splitting claims that false arithmetic statement or the valid ambient one.','LMSfirstpreprint2011,published2014; manuscript-specific intended group unresolved.','Origin-dependent ambient splitting established; intended finite arithmetic extension still a reading/identification issue.',['LMS2014: §2, formulas2.2–2.7 and paragraph followingLemma2.5'], 'medium')
c(24,[30],[(23,762,786)],'«E+∧E−=1, R′∧R′′=1, R²=1 … E+⁶=1»','For nonzero a, K(a)=lim{x:xⁿ=a} is a torsor under T=limμ_n, and root multiplication gives K(a)∧_T K(b)≅K(ab). Thus K(−j)K(−jbar)=K(1), K(1/2)K(2)=K(1), K(−1)²=K(1), and K(−j)^6=K(1). Inversion exchanges the paired fibres. The two centre fibres separately are G_Q(μ3)-stable; G_Q exchanges them, so they are not individually G_Q-torsors without changing the base.','proof sketched',{'program':'programs/computations.py','output':'outputs/computations.json:C16','method':'Multiplication/inversion and(-j)^6=1 checked exactly. The subgroup restriction is an explicit modern qualification of the source’s geometric torsor language.'},'E','Contracted products of torsors and Kummer classes are standard cohomological operations; Milne’s H¹ and Kummer formulations supply the exact field/base distinction.','Classical Kummer/cohomology before manuscript.','Geometric torsor identities true; arithmetic equivariance must use the stabilizer of each algebraic parameter.',['MilneCFT2020: II Example1.18; VII AppendixA'], 'high')
c(25,[36,38],[(25,790,830),(44,1682,1689)],'«C_n:xⁿ+yⁿ+zⁿ=0»; «F=2n² … S=3n»; «g=(n−1)(n−2)/2»','Over an algebraically closed field with char not dividingn, the smooth Fermat curve C_n maps to C_1 by nth powers, degree n², deckΠ_n=μ_n³/diagμ_n. Over each of0,1,∞ it has n points of indexn; away from them it is a Π_n-torsor. OverC its pulled-back triangular decomposition has V=3n,E=3n²,F=2n², giving genus(n−1)(n−2)/2. The printed examples n=1,2,3,4,6,12 give0,0,1,3,10,55.','proof sketched',{'program':'programs/computations.py','output':'outputs/computations.json:C07','method':'Smoothness by partial derivatives, degree and deckgroup by ratios, Riemann–Hurwitz2g−2=−2n²+3n(n−1), and independent Euler calculation agree identically.'},'E','Fermat-cover and smooth-plane-curve formulas are classical. SGA1 XII permits topological cover/Euler comparison in characteristic zero; the ramification computation gives the tame algebraic formula independently. Ihara1986 is a later arithmetic development of related Fermat towers, not the origin of these elementary counts.','Classical before the manuscript; Ihara1986 publication for later related work.','All displayed genus/count claims correct under char∤n; triangulation itself refers to a chosen complex realization.',['SGA1: XII5.1–5.2','Ihara1986: bibliographic comparison only'], 'high')
c(26,[37,39],[(26,835,873)],'«C_nd→C_n»; «T=limμ_n»; «π∞=T³/T»','For d,n≥1, coordinatewise dth powers define C_nd→C_n. These compatible finite covers form the Fermat tower. Its inverse-limit deck group is T³/diagT (rank2 overZhat in characteristic zero). The diagram’s apparent arrow reversal at line838 conflicts with the written powers; a quotient description at each finite level passes to the limit using the compatible diagonal splitting or coordinates.','proof sketched',{'program':'programs/computations.py','output':'outputs/computations.json:C07,C16','method':'(x^d)^n=x^(dn), quotient by simultaneous scalars, and two coordinate ratios provide the exact transition/deck formulas.'},'E','Classical Kummer cover towers and inverse limits. SGA1 V and XII provide the general finite-cover framework; no published exact-line emendation is asserted.','Classical before manuscript.','Correct with arrows C_nd→C_n and inverse-limit interpretation.',['SGA1: V and XII5.1–5.2'], 'high')
c(27,[40],[(27,877,914),(28,919,937)],'«S3×Γ … par homothéties parχ»','Coordinate permutations act on the Fermat tower and induce S3 on the three branch labels. G_Q acts onΠ_n through the cyclotomic characterχ_n and onM=T³/T by scalarχ; these actions commute. Together with deck transformations this supplies (S3×G_Q)⋉M as an acting group. This is a constructed action, not automatically the full unmarked curve automorphism group.','proof sketched',{'program':'programs/computations.py','output':'outputs/computations.json:C08,C09,C16','method':'Conjugating coordinate multiplications sends each root of unityζ toζ^χ. Permutations commute with the scalar operation; semidirect multiplication follows.'},'E','Elementary Kummer descent with cyclotomic coefficients; the later Ihara representations involve deeper quotients and Fermat homology, so their existence does not promote this scalar action to a faithful action ofG_Q.','Classical Kummer theory; relevant Ihara papers published1986.','Correct for the explicitly constructed action.',['MilneCFT2020: VII AppendixA','SGA1: IX6.1','Ihara1986: bibliography only'], 'high')
c(28,[41],[(28,938,947)],'«Aut_Q(C_n)≃(S3×Γ)⋉μ_n»; «Aut_Q(C_1)=S3×Γ»','The literal unmarked automorphism formulas cannot be right: C_1≅P¹ has geometric automorphism groupPGL₂, notS3, and the Fermat deck group isΠ_n of ordern², notμ_n of ordern. For the cover tower with its map to the three-point marked base, the constructed deck/permutation/semilinear group is(S3×G_Q)⋉Π_n. This reconstruction concerns compatible cover automorphisms; it does not assert all automorphisms of each proper C_n.','false as stated',{'program':'programs/computations.py','output':'outputs/computations.json:C15,C07','counterexample':'n=1: t↦t+2 is an invertible automorphism ofP¹_Q, inverse t↦t−2, and does not preserve{0,1,∞}; it satisfies the literal unmarked input. The rank discrepancy n² versusn is independent.'},'C','Marked-cover automorphism groups are standard SGA1 cover theory. The counterexample refutes the literal statement; a literature result on full automorphism groups of Fermat curves is unnecessary and would require exceptional-degree distinctions.','Classical before manuscript; literal discrepancy rechecked2026-11-19.','False for unmarked curves; missing stars/marking andΠ_n-versusμ_n are scan questions.',['SGA1: V Galois-cover correspondence'], 'high')
c(29,[42],[(28,949,962),(29,963,971)],'«π₁(Cbar_1*)ab≃π∞»','For U=P¹_Qbar−{0,1,∞}, the Fermat power maps form a cofinal family in its finite geometric abelian covers, andπ₁(U)^ab≅T³/diagT≅Zhat(1)², with inertia classes summing to0. Thus the source studies the maximal geometric abelian cover; it does not identify the full free profinite groupπ₁(U) with this module.','stated without proof',{'method':'Complex comparison givesπ₁^top(U)=F2 and abelianizationZ²; the Fermat quotient at leveln has degree n² and independent inertia generators, hence equals the modn abelian quotient. Every finite abelian quotient factors through one of these.','output':'Independent proof; finite degrees checked C07.'},'E','SGA1 XII5.1–5.2 plus the elementary punctured-sphere presentation proves the exact statement. Esquisse§2’s full outerGalois action is a separate object; Ihara1986’s Fermat-Jacobian action is also a separate representation.','Classical topology and SGA1 seminar1960–1961/publication1971.','True. G_Q acts throughχ on this quotient, so it loses the kernel of the cyclotomic character.',['SGA1: XII5.1–5.2','Esquisse1984: §2'], 'high')
c(30,[43],[(29,975,1002)],'«{yⁿ+zⁿ=0}/μ_n … (y/z)ⁿ=−1»','For the branch value indexed by the vanishing coordinate, the projective fibre is canonically a torsor of nth roots of−1 via the ratio of the other two coordinates. Before taking a ratio, nonzero affine pairs must be quotiented by allG_m, not onlyμ_n; the literal unnormalized quotient byμ_n remains infinite.','false as stated',{'program':'programs/computations.py','output':'outputs/computations.json:C15','counterexample':'n=1: {(y,z)≠(0,0):y+z=0}/μ1 has infinitely many points overQbar, whereas its projectivization and the curve fibre have exactlyone point.'},'C','The corrected projective ratio description is elementary homogeneous geometry/Kummer theory. No evidence was found that the draft intended to assert an infinite projective fibre; a missing normalization is plausible but undecided without scans.','Classical geometry; literal quotient refuted2026-11-19.','The ratio formula and torsor are correct; the displayed quotient requires normalization orG_m.',['MilneCFT2020: VII AppendixA, torsor background'], 'high')
c(31,[43],[(30,1027,1054)],'«M —2→ M→π₂»; «E_i=p⁻¹(i)/Z_i»','Let M=T^I/diagT and identifyI with the three nonzero classes ofM/2M. PutZ_i⊂M for the rank-one inertia subgroup. The cosetp⁻¹(i) is stable under the action ofM through multiplication by2; quotient byZ_i for that scaled action is equivalently quotient by2Z_i inM. This gives the branch-fibre M-set with stabilizerZ_i. Ordinary unscaled translation does not preservep⁻¹(i).','proof sketched',{'program':'programs/computations.py','output':'outputs/computations.json:C11','method':'Finite-level coset/stabilizer counts at mod2m and general scaled-action argument. Division by2 identifies the subgroup2M withM as compact groups; the coset is a torsor under2M, not a subgroup.'},'E','The result is an elementary quotient-torsor construction; SGA1’s subgroup/cover dictionary supplies the language. Its exact action convention is encoded by the source’s displayed×2 arrow, so no new unproved theorem remains.','Classical group/cover theory; exact finite-level recheck2026-11-19.','Correct when the action is read through×2.',['SGA1: V Galois categories, background'], 'high')
c(32,[43],[(30,1005,1026),(30,1044,1048)],'«π₂*=I»; marginal «trois points rat/Q» on uncertainCbar∞/Cbar₂','The nonzero vectors ofM/2M form the natural3-element S3-set, and G_Q acts trivially mod2. In contrast, the marginal literal assertion of rational points onC_2:x²+y²+z²=0 is false: it has no real, hence noQ, points. An inverse-limit curve mapping toC_2 has noQ-point either. A different intended curve/label remains possible.','false as stated',{'program':'programs/computations.py','output':'outputs/computations.json:C08,C15','counterexample':'OverQ⊂R, a sum ofthree squares vanishes only when allthree coordinates arezero, which is excluded inP². The6 coordinate permutations realizeGL₂(F₂) andverify the correct main assertion.'},'C','The finite linear action is standard; the anisotropic conic is an elementary arithmetic obstruction. The source’s uncertain margin is treated as a reading alarm rather than attribution of a settled authorial error.','Classical algebra; literal-margin check2026-11-19.','Main mod2 statement true; proposed rational-point reading impossible for either indicated curve.',['MilneCFT2020: elementary field background only'], 'high')
c(33,[44,46],[(31,1059,1105),(32,1115,1154),(33,1157,1192),(34,1204,1216)],'«Isom(I,μ3)»; «le quotient … deux éléments»; «M —3→ M→π3»','In characteristic zero the six bijectionsI→μ3 modulo diagonalμ3 form a2-element setω corresponding to the two centres−j,−jbar. At leveln the cone oftriples inμ3n³ with distinct nth powers has6n³ elements; quotient by diagonalμ3n has2n² and identifies with the two Fermat fibres. In the inverse limitp3⁻¹(ω) carries the M-action through×3, freely on each residue coset. Several earlier cubing/cone definitions are visibly deleted.','corrected by himself later (both places): failed cone/cubing attempts at scan31 lines1083–1105 replaced by six-bijection/cone construction scans32–34 lines1115–1216',{'program':'programs/computations.py','output':'outputs/computations.json:C10,C16','method':'Exact counts forn1…7 supplement general proof:6 choices of orderedμ3-values, n³ lifts, free scalar orbit ofsize3n. nth-power substitution gives the Fermat equation. ×3 action preserves residue cosets.'},'E','The coherent final construction is finite torus/root-of-unity algebra, an explicit instance of standard Kummer-torsor theory. Searches found no need for a later theorem; inconsistent surviving exponents are isolated in readings rather than normalized silently.','Classical Kummer theory; draft revisions undated.','Correct final construction after specifying3n,n and the quotient action; undecided glyphs still need scans.',['MilneCFT2020: VII AppendixA'], 'high')
c(34,[45],[(34,1218,1226)],'«une droite invariante … unique»','OnV=F₃³/diagF₃, a3-cycle acts with a unique invariant line. Its nonzero vectors areω; a transposition acts by−1. For the arithmetic coefficient module the action on this line is the product ofthe permutation sign andχ3.','stated without proof',{'program':'programs/computations.py','output':'outputs/computations.json:C09','method':'MatrixA=[[0,−1],[1,−1]] has characteristicpolynomial(X−1)² overF3 andrank(A−I)=1; the four projective lines are exhaustively checked andonlyspan(1,−1) survives. The sign×χ3 assertion is this run’s explicit deduction.'},'E','Elementary modular representation theory ofS3; exact line/character calculation re-proved here. Broader later CM-parity literature cannot identify the external project character without an additional comparison map.','Classical finite linear algebra; explicit reconstruction2026-11-19.','Correct; manuscript states uniqueness, while the sign-character formulation is the earlier-run reconstruction independently verified.',['MilneCFT2020: cyclotomic/Kummer background only'], 'high')
c(35,[47],[(35,1230,1295)],'«α′(0)=2, α′(1)=−1 … 1/2»; «K(a) … a∈Qbar»','The three midpoint values are2,−1,1/2, and the Fermat fibre over the midpoint oppositei isK_n(−1/2)^(I minus{i}). For nonzeroa,b, contracted multiplicationK(a)∧K(b)=K(ab) yieldsK(−1/2)=K(−1)∧K(2)^−1. The written all-a quantifier must exclude0: K_n(0) is not aμ_n-torsor forn>1. The third label written2 conflicts withI={0,1,∞}.','false as stated',{'program':'programs/computations.py','output':'outputs/computations.json:C15,C16,C17','method':'Projective coordinate substitution, root multiplication, andzero-fibre cardinality. Main midpoint construction is correct; only literal parameter scope/index is false.'},'C','The corrected statements are standard Kummer-torsor arithmetic. Searches matched the general formalism but no independent source for the draft’s coordinate label; ink must decide the intended∞ versus2.','Classical Kummer theory; exact label/scope recheck2026-11-19.','Correct for nonzero parameters and the standardthree-label set; literalzero case false.',['MilneCFT2020: VII AppendixA'], 'high')

c(36,[48],[(36,1300,1347),(37,1350,1360)],'«A=Aff(T,K(1/2))»; «les opérations … à travers»','The arithmetic actions on the explicitly listed branch, centre and midpoint fibres factor through the affine action on the Kummer torsorK(1/2), together with S3 and the deck moduleM=T³/T. The linear part ofA acts onM by its scalar character; translations affect the midpoint torsor. A chosen origin identifiesA withZhat⋊Zhat*. This factorization concerns these specified quotient sets, not all étale covers or the full outerGalois action.','proof sketched',{'program':'programs/computations.py','output':'outputs/computations.json:C16','method':'Each fibre is expressed in root-of-unity modules and powers/inverses ofK(1/2); the affine law and dual sign are checked symbolically. No faithful fullG_Q representation is inferred.'},'E','The action factorization follows from classical Kummer-torsor functoriality; LMS2014§2 supplies a precise modern ambient affine group for rational radicals. The later exact-image theorem answers claim037, while the formal factorization itself is elementary.','Classical Kummer formalism; LMSpreprint2011/publication2014 for the exactimage treatment.','Correct at the stated quotient level.',['MilneCFT2020: II Example1.18; VII AppendixA','LMS2014: §2'], 'high')
c(37,[31],[(20,643,649),(36,1342,1347)],'«image … d’indice fini? … surjectif?»','For θ=(2^(1/n)) compatible and chosen standard compatible roots ofunity, write g(ζ_n)=ζ_n^a andg(θ_n)=ζ_n^bθ_n. The full image inA=Zhat⋊Zhat* is exactly{(a,b): (−1)^b=(2/a)}, where(2/a) is the conductor8 quadratic Dirichlet character. It hasindex2, so the finite-index question has a positive answer and surjectivity a negative one. ReplacingK(2) byK(1/2) changesb to−b, preserving the parity constraint.','open on the pages',{'program':'programs/computations.py','output':'outputs/computations.json:C04,C16','method':'Independent level8 certificate: θ_8⁴=√2=ζ8+ζ8⁻¹ forces theparityrelation; Eisenstein plus adjoining i givesfielddegree16 againstambient32. Full-tower sufficiency is the exact theoremLMS2014, not a finite-level inference.'},'B','LMS2014 Theorem2.9 identifies the radical Galoisgroup as thekernel ofa quadratic character ofindex2; forr0=2 thecharacter is exactly thedisplayedcondition. Lemma2.5 proves the intersection usingSchinzel1977Theorem2; Remark2.12.2 isolates the2-adic obstruction for±2. NoGRH is used in this algebraic image theorem.','LMSfirstcirculation arXiv20Dec2011; finalarXiv25Aug2014; publishedonline5Nov2014 and journal157(2014),489–511. Earlier Schinzel1977 criterion is an ancestor; first historical explicit formulation ofthis exactimage not claimed.','Settled exactly and unconditionally; the arithmetic extension overZhat* is not the same split extension as ambientA.',['LMS2014: Lemma2.5, Theorem2.9, Remark2.12(2)','Schinzel1977: verifiedbibliographicancestor, fulltextblocked'], 'high')
c(38,[49],[(15,424,437),(37,1362,1372)],'«les développements précédents … n’importe quel corps premier … n premiers à la car.»','The Fermat maps and maximal prime-to-p geometric abelian tower survive overF_p withnprime top. However all preceding special-fibre constructions do not survive unchanged in characteristics2or3: midpoint1/2 isundefined at2, and the two centres coalesce at3 becausez²−z+1=(z+1)². One must also distinguish the full abelianπ1 incharacteristicp, which haswildp-covers, fromits prime-to-p quotient. Adelic number-field finite-index image claims do not transfer toG_Fp, a procyclicgroup.','false as stated',{'program':'programs/computations.py','output':'outputs/computations.json:C13','counterexample':'Characteristic2 withn=1 satisfiesnprime top but1/2 isundefined. Characteristic3 withn=1 satisfiesnprime top butμ3 hasonlyone geometricpoint andtwo centres collapse. Characteristicp affine-line Artin–Schreier covers refute anunqualified full-cover extrapolation.'},'C','SGA1 XIII Corollaire2.12 gives the prime-to-p fundamental-group framework; the displayedFermat derivative/ramification computation establishes thetame core. The specialfibres requirechar≠2,3. No generalfinite-field analogue ofthenumber-field affineopenimage statement isclaimed.','SGA1 publication1971; geometric ingredientsclassical; precise scope correction2026-11-19.','Core tameconstruction correct; literalblanketgeneralization falsewithouttheextra exclusions and arithmeticqualification.',['SGA1: XIII Corollaire2.12; XII5.1–5.2 forchar0'], 'high')
c(39,[53],[(38,1397,1427)],'«ζ_n=exp(2iπ/3n)»; «base canonique»','The systemζ_n belongs tolim_nμ3n, not componentwise toμ_n. Cofinal reindexing canonically identifieslimμ3n withT=limμ_n by(v_n)↦(v_n³), inverse(u_n)↦(u3n). The complex exponential system then corresponds totheusualgenerator ofT. Thegenerator isrelative tothechosenembedding intoC and itsorientation; itisnot anabsolute algebraicchoice.','stated without proof',{'program':'programs/computations.py','output':'outputs/computations.json:C16','method':'The two maps compose toidentity bycompatibilityv3n³=v_n andu3n³=u_n. Directcoordinates ruleout a componentwiseμ_n reading.'},'E','Cofinal inverse limits and the complex description ofroots ofunity arestandard. No literature hitwas needed toinvent anemendation3n→n; theexactcofinal maps prove acoherentreading ofthegiven formula.','Classical topology/algebra beforemanuscript.','Correct with cofinal reindexing; theliteraltermwiseμ_n membership wouldbefalse.',['SGA1: V inverse-limit/profinite background'], 'high')
c(40,[54],[(31,1059,1081),(38,1383,1420),(39,1434,1456)],'«α=−jbar»; «(−1,+1,0)»','Witht=−xⁿ/zⁿ andζ_n=exp(2πi/3n), α_n=(ζbar_n,ζ_n,1) has nthpowers(jbar,j,1), hence liesonC_n overα=−jbar. Complexconjugation exchanges thetwocentres andnegates thevector(−1,1,0) inM. The opposite naming−j inearliercontext isaconventiondifference, not grounds tochange thissource.','proof sketched',{'program':'programs/computations.py','output':'outputs/computations.json:C12','method':'j²+j+1=0 andcoordinateconjugation; exactroot-systemsubstitution.'},'E','Elementary complexroots-ofunity arithmetic. The comparison toexternalCMparityprojects lacks acheckedframe map; itdoesnot change themanuscript’s internallyconsistentchoice.','Classical cyclotomy; explicitrunrecheck2026-11-19.','Correct; coordinateorder andcentrelabel mustremain synchronized.',['MilneCFT2020: VII AppendixA, cyclotomicbackground'], 'high')
c(41,[52],[(37,1368,1372),(39,1458,1477)],'«ρ0ρ1ρ∞=1»; «donnent l’isom …»','The threecoordinateclassesρ_i inM=Zhat³/diagZhat obeyρ0+ρ1+ρ∞=0 andgenerateM. They aretheperipheral generators oftheabelianized three-punctured-spheregroup. The displayedcartographic sourcegroupsubscript isuncertain; an isomorphismfromthefullnonabelian groupcannotfollow.','open on the pages',{'method':'Exactmodulequotientcomputation; comparisonwiththetopological peripheralpresentation underSGA1XII.','output':'The precise source-group glyph remains aStage4reading; noinkdecision.'},'C','The abelianizedperipheral identification isclassical andproved bySGA1comparison plusF2abelianization. Esquisse§3–4 latergives thecartographiccover correspondence, butdoesnot identifythis uncertainwritten sourcegroup.','SGA1seminar1960–1961/publication1971; Esquisse1984/1997.','Underlyingabelianclaim established; manuscript’s precisegroupsymbol unidentifiablefromthetranscription.',['SGA1: XII5.1–5.2','Esquisse1984: §§3–4'], 'high')
c(42,[55],[(40,1482,1512)],'«x↦ax+b»; «x↦ax−b»','Afterchoosing acompatiblerootoriginθ of2, itsKummer torsorhasaffineGaloisactionx↦χ(g)x+b(g). Theinverse torsorK(1/2), withoriginθ⁻¹, hastranslation−b. The lawis(a,b)(c,d)=(ac,b+ad), andchangingorigin addsacoboundary. Positivityofθ isnotawrittenhypothesis.','proof sketched',{'program':'programs/computations.py','output':'outputs/computations.json:C16','method':'Coordinateinversion and symboliccomposition prove thesignandcocyclelaw.'},'E','Classical Kummer/crossed-homomorphismformalism, explicitlygiveninMilneIIExample1.18; LMS2014uses thecorresponding affineambientgroup.','Classical beforemanuscript; LMS2011/2014modernexactimagedescription.','Correct; thethetaorigins arechoices, notcanonicallyarithmeticobjects.',['MilneCFT2020: II Example1.18','LMS2014: §2'], 'high')
c(43,[55],[(40,1514,1534),(41,1538,1557)],'«ces18flèches … deux d’entre elles»; Γdeleted→«S3×{1,τ}»','The manuscript proposes18 transports among itscentre, midpoint andbranchfibres andasserts that deckequivarianceplusS3×{1,complexconjugation} symmetries reduce them totwo representatives. Acompletedpathsystem mustalso satisfy stabilizercompatibility and compositionrelations. Those conditions andthetwocompletearrows arenotprovided inthispacket. GeneralG_Q-equivariance isexplicitly retracted.','open on the pages',{'method':'Readthewhole18-arrowdiagram andcontinuation; distinguish combinatorialorbitreduction from existence/consistency oftransports. One displayedrepresentativeformula ischecked separately inclaim044.','output':'No claimed proof or exhaustiveorbitcertificate for18→2 because intendedpathchoices andarrowlabels areincomplete.'},'C','Publisheddessin/Fermat-tower andIhara-related sources supply establishedcover/pathformalisms, but targetedsearches didnotlocate theseexact18maps withthischoiceofcentres andmidpoints. The symmetryreduction isplausible inawell-specified groupoid; its unpublishedcoherence remainsunfinished, not aglobalnewopenproblem.','Ihara1986 andEsquisse1984/1997 relatedframeworks; noexactsettlementidentified asof2026-11-19.','Unfinishedlocalconstruction. Worth a bounded reconstruction onlyaftertheformulaandglyphalarms areresolved.',['SGA1: V fibre functors and paths','Esquisse1984: §§3–4','Ihara1986: bibliography verified, exactpath claim notchecked'], 'medium')
